{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790271640,"nonce":"B+LnhM3AR3Nig0JIKgSOm9w6Y+4=","title":"Python: a local read before its only binding reads the global once rebind versions it","message":"rebind.rs treats a name as rebound when its first occurrence (a read) comes before the assignment, so a function whose only store to G follows a read of G renames that store and every later occurrence to G$1. G then has no store left in the function, and the earlier read resolves to the module global instead of raising UnboundLocalError.\n\n    G = 5\n    def f():\n        try:\n            print(G)\n        except Exception:\n            print(\"unbound\")\n        G = \"seven\"\n        return G\n    print(f())\n\npython3 prints \"unbound\", \"seven\". The candidate after d39b2847 prints 5, seven (dea6d08b printed null, also wrong: it never raises UnboundLocalError). is_candidate should require an earlier binding (a parameter or a store), not an earlier occurrence.","files":null}]}